# Unsupervised-taxonomy manuscript, part 2: methods (mathematics), usage guide, caveats, conclusions, references.

METHOD = [
    {"t": "h1", "text": "3. Method and mathematics"},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "3.1 Compositional data and why ordinary statistics fail on it"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Tank-waste compositions are compositional data: each sample is described by the relative amounts of a set of constituents, and the information lives in the ratios between constituents, not in any absolute amount. A sludge reported as 40 percent iron and 10 percent aluminum carries the same compositional information as one reported as 400 and 100 grams per kilogram, or 4 and 1 pounds per cubic foot. Formally, a composition is a point on the simplex, the set of positive vectors whose parts sum to a constant (conventionally 1 or 100 percent). Three consequences follow, and each one invalidates a naive approach. First, closure: because the parts sum to a constant, every part is determined by all the others, which makes raw component values structurally negative-correlated; the correlation between two analytes across tanks is distorted by the sum constraint before any chemistry is expressed. Second, the parts-whole relationship: increasing one component necessarily decreases all others even if nothing else changed, so distance measures computed on raw fractions respond to dilution (how much water is in the tank) rather than to chemistry. Third, Euclidean geometry is the wrong geometry: fractions live between 0 and 1, differences near 1 are bounded while differences near 0 are unbounded in ratio terms, and a change from 0.5 percent to 0.6 percent (a 20 percent relative change, chemically large) is numerically identical to a change from 50.0 to 50.1 percent (a 0.2 percent relative change, chemically trivial). Mixing units makes this worse: kilograms of sodium, curies of cesium, and micrograms per gram of titanium are mutually incommensurable, so any Euclidean distance over raw columns is an arbitrary weighting of units."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The standard remedy, established by Aitchison in the 1980s, is to work in log-ratio coordinates, where ratios become differences and scale factors cancel exactly. Because log of a ratio is invariant to multiplying both numerator and denominator by any constant, converting compositions to log-ratios removes the unknown density, moisture, and unit scale that would otherwise dominate a cross-site comparison. All distances in this paper are computed after such a transform."},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "3.2 The centered log-ratio transform"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Let a sample have closed fractions $f_j$ over features $j$ (each feature divided by the sample's total across panel features, so the fractions sum to 1). The centered log-ratio (CLR) transform maps the composition to a vector of the same dimension:"},
    {"t": "eq", "text": "CLR$_j$ = ln $f_j$ - (1/D) $\\Sigma_k$ ln $f_k$,   j = 1..D"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "that is, each log fraction minus the mean log fraction, which is the log of the geometric mean $g$ of the fractions: CLR$_j$ = ln($f_j$/$g$). Figure 7 shows the transform on real data: Sludge Batch 11 supernate closed over its salt-panel species, before and after. The CLR vector has a constraint (its entries sum to exactly zero) and a singularity (any zero fraction gives minus infinity), which drive the two design choices below. The zero-sum constraint means CLR coordinates live in a D-minus-1-dimensional hyperplane through the origin; distance computations that center differences on the mean of the difference (as ours do, below) are insensitive to this constraint. The singularity is the practical problem: tank-waste data is full of non-detects, and the transform must be combined with a principled treatment of zeros."},
    {"t": "fig", "file": "fig_clr_concept.png", "caption": "Figure 7. The CLR transform on real data: Sludge Batch 11 supernate closed over the salt-panel species (A), and the same sample in CLR coordinates (B). The geometric mean of the closed fractions sets the zero line; a species' CLR value is how many times (in log terms) its fraction exceeds that mean. Large negative bars are species barely above the closure floor, not absent species: true zeros cannot be transformed at all, which is why the pipeline never lets them in."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Why not alternatives: an additive log-ratio (ALR) transform takes logs of ratios to one chosen reference part, which is simpler but makes distances depend on which part is chosen as the reference; an isometric log-ratio (ILR) transform builds orthonormal coordinates that preserve geometry exactly, at the cost of coordinates that no longer correspond one-to-one to analytes, which would destroy per-analyte interpretation of cluster differences. CLR is the standard compromise: coordinates remain analyte-wise interpretable, and its only geometric defect (a slight distortion from the zero-sum constraint) is irrelevant for the centroid-based and nearest-neighbor comparisons used here because our distance explicitly recenters differences."},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "3.3 Non-detects, the neutral point, and the presence floor"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "A reported zero in tank-waste data almost never means 'this substance is absent'; it means 'not measured, or below the detection limit of the method'. Substituting zero into a log-ratio is catastrophic (minus infinity); substituting a small arbitrary value distorts every distance by an amount that depends on the arbitrary choice. The pipeline instead uses a neutral-point imputation: an unreported analyte is treated as if its fraction equalled the geometric mean of the analytes that were reported. The rationale is exact in CLR space: a neutral point contributes exactly zero to the CLR vector, so an unmeasured analyte pulls neither toward nor away from any comparison; it is the 'no information' element of the log-ratio geometry. This is the correct null when the analyst's state of knowledge is 'unknown' rather than 'zero'."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Two safeguards complete the treatment. A presence floor requires an analyte to be reported by at least a fraction of Hanford points (0.40 for sludge, 0.70 for salt, 0.50 for radionuclides) to enter the panel at all; analytes kept alive by a handful of reports would otherwise manufacture clusters of convenience. Figure 8 shows how the feature count falls as the floor rises, and that the chosen floors sit on the flat shoulder of each curve rather than at a cliff edge. A sample may enter a panel only if it shares at least two reportable features with it (the minimum needed to center and compare a difference vector); this rule is what makes 'cannot be projected' a meaningful output rather than an error."},
    {"t": "fig", "file": "fig_presence.png", "caption": "Figure 8. Feature retention versus presence floor. Stars mark the floors used (0.40 sludge, 0.70 salt, 0.50 radionuclides). The chosen floors sit on gentle slopes, so small changes in floor choice would not materially change the panels."},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "3.4 Distances: Aitchison and the shared-support variant"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The natural distance between two CLR vectors x and y is the Aitchison distance, which in CLR coordinates reduces to the Euclidean distance after removing the component along the all-ones direction (which the zero-sum constraint already fixes): $d_A(x,y) = ( \\Sigma_j ( (x_j - y_j) - m )^2 )^{1/2}$ with $m$ the mean of the differences. The pipeline uses a shared-support variant: for a pair of samples where one lacks some of the other's panel features, the difference vector is formed only over the features both report (the shared support, size k), centered on its own mean, and summed. This is what makes cross-site distances legitimate at all: an SRS filtrate reporting 9 nuclides and a Hanford supernatant reporting 21 can still be compared on their 9 common features, with k reported alongside the distance as its own quality flag. The geometric meaning is the same (a path length in log-ratio space); the caveat is that distances computed on different supports are not strictly commensurable, which is why k travels with every distance in every table, and why an analog at moderate distance on large k outranks a closer analog on small k."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Two properties make this the right family of distances for the audit purpose. Scale invariance: multiplying a sample's reported values by any constant (a density correction, a unit conversion, a reporting convention) leaves every distance unchanged, which removes the largest source of cross-site bookkeeping error. Subcompositional coherence: comparing samples on a subset of features is internally consistent with comparing them on the full set, so a panel boundary (sludge versus salt chemistry) is a legitimate analysis choice rather than a distortion."},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "3.5 Clustering, model selection, and the honesty metrics"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Ward's method (Ward 1963) is agglomerative hierarchical clustering with a specific merge cost: at each step, merge the pair of clusters whose fusion increases the within-cluster sum of squared distances the least. On CLR coordinates it behaves like a variance-minimizing, roughly spherical-partition method, which suits compositional continua with islands. It produces a full dendrogram; cutting it at K merges gives the taxonomy. Three diagnostics accompany it. The cophenetic correlation (Sokal and Rohlf 1962) is the correlation between the pairwise distances in the original space and the heights at which pairs merge in the tree; values run 0.59 to 0.68 here, meaning the tree is a moderate compression of a blobby space, a caution against reading the dendrogram's exact branching as genealogy. The silhouette score (Rousseeuw 1987) places each point between -1 and 1 by comparing its mean distance to its own cluster against its mean distance to the nearest other cluster; means of 0.19 to 0.29 indicate overlapping clusters, real structure but soft boundaries. The adjusted Rand index (Hubert and Arabie 1985) measures agreement between two partitions of the same points (here Ward's K clusters against KMeans' on the same K), corrected for chance; values of 0.71 to 0.79 mean the two very different algorithms recover substantially the same families, which is the strongest internal evidence the taxonomy has."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "KMeans (Lloyd's algorithm, k-means++ initialization, 50 restarts) serves two roles: as the agreement check just described, and in a sweep over k = 2 to 12 whose silhouette scores select K. The sweep is constrained to the interpretable window k = 4 to 9, because the goal is a taxonomy a human can reason about cluster by cluster, and because the silhouette differences within the window are small enough that interpretability should break the tie. HDBSCAN (Campello et al. 2013) is fit not for its partition (its cluster counts, 4 to 7 per panel, are reported but not used downstream) but for its density estimates; UMAP (McInnes et al. 2018) is used only for the figures."},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "3.6 Density anomaly scoring and its failure mode here"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "HDBSCAN builds a density estimate from the mutual reachability distance between points, persists clusters across density levels, and scores each point with GLOSH (Global-Local Outlier Score from Hierarchies, Campello et al. 2015): the estimated probability that a point is an outlier, 0 to 1, high values meaning the local density around the point is much lower than the density of the cluster it would belong to. In the radionuclide panel, where feature coverage is decent and one dense supercluster dominates, the scores behave as designed and exactly one point crosses the 0.85 flag line. In the element panels, however, 55 percent of points cross it, and the reason is a known interaction between GLOSH and sparse high-dimensional data: when most points are missing most features (imputed to the neutral point), a point's effective neighborhood is defined by whichever few features it reports, local densities become individually calibrated, and the outlier score partly measures 'how unusual my missing-data pattern is' rather than 'how unusual my chemistry is'. The correct operational stance is asymmetric trust, stated in Section 5 as a rule: radionuclide-panel flags are evidence; element-panel flags are prompts."},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "3.7 UMAP and what its pictures do and do not show"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "UMAP constructs a fuzzy graph of k-nearest-neighbor relationships in the high-dimensional space and optimizes a low-dimensional layout to preserve that graph. In these figures it is fitted on the Hanford CLR matrix of the relevant panel, and the SRS samples are then mapped with the fitted model (the transform operation, not a refit), so an SRS star's position is the location the Hanford structure assigns it, which is exactly the audit we want. Three warnings attach to every such figure, and they apply here. Distances between well-separated groups in a UMAP plot are not meaningful (only local neighborhoods are approximately preserved); relative point sizes and gaps within a cluster can exaggerate density differences; and the same dataset gives different-looking maps under changed random seeds, which is why every quantitative statement in this paper references CLR-space distances and percentiles, never pixel distances in Figure 1. The plots are for orientation; the numbers are for decisions."},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "3.8 The projection operation, precisely"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "For each SRS sample and panel, projection means: (1) close the sample over the panel's kept features; (2) impute unreported features to the neutral point; (3) take CLR coordinates; (4) compute the shared-support Aitchison distance to each Ward cluster centroid, over the features the sample reports; (5) assign the sample to the nearest centroid's cluster; (6) report the distance percentile of the sample within that cluster's own members (same shared-support metric, per member); (7) rank all cluster members as candidate analogs by shared-support distance and list the top five with their k. Closure residuals pass through the identical steps. No Hanford or SRS label ever enters steps 1 through 7; the only site-aware step is the decision to run an SRS sample through a Hanford-fitted taxonomy, which is the paper's premise."},
]

GUIDE = [
    {"t": "h1", "text": "4. How to use the results"},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "4.1 Reading a projection"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Every SRS row in Table 2 carries five numbers, and each answers one question. Which cluster: the family of Hanford compositions the stream most resembles overall; treat it as a pointer to a group of tanks to read about, not as a classification. Centroid distance: how far the stream sits from that family's center in log-ratio terms; interpret it against the calibration below, never in the abstract. Percentile within cluster: the same distance expressed against the cluster's own members; below 95 means inside the spread Hanford itself displays, above 95 means on the rim or outside. Top-analog distance and support k: the distance to the single closest Hanford tank-phase and how many features that comparison rests on. The reading order is: k first (is the comparison even adequately supported?), then the percentile (is this distance ordinary for this cluster?), then the identity of the analog (does its process history make chemical sense as a relative?), and only last the raw distance. A stream such as Salt Batch 13 (radionuclide panel) reads: k=8, 98th percentile of C6, top analog C-108 sludge mixed-phase at 3.4, second analog AN-104 supernatant; the conclusion is 'radiologically akin to late Hanford supernate-bearing wastes, with the nearest single-tank match in a mixed-phase tank', and the next analytical step is a targeted analyte-by-analyte comparison of those specific pairs, which the dashboard's Dataspace tab renders directly."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "4.2 Calibration rules of thumb"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The null context of Figure 6 gives concrete reading rules. In the radionuclide panel, an analog distance under about 3 is as close as Hanford tanks get to each other (call it a strong analog); 3 to 9 is within the range Hanford's own variety produces (a moderate analog, evaluate k and chemistry); above about 9, the analog is weaker than typical Hanford intra-cluster variation (treat as directional only). In the salt panel the same thresholds tighten to roughly 1.5 and 4; in the sludge panel to roughly 3.3 and 6.4. A distance below the panel's Hanford median with k of 7 or more is the strongest claim the method can make (Salt Batch 12 at 2.6 on k=9 is the exemplar). A distance above the 90th percentile with k under 5 is the weakest (SB11's element-panel analog at 5.3 on k=4 must be treated as indicative). Support size can rescue a moderate distance (batches 8 and 9 at 5.4 to 6.7 on k=19 are numerically better-supported than a 3.0 on k=3) but cannot make a novel stream familiar: the PRFT streams fail at 11.8 to 12.2 regardless of support because the chemistry itself (added titanate sorbent) has no Hanford referent."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "4.3 What each finding is good for"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The genuine-analog finding (salt batches 10-13 in radionuclide space) supports direct reuse of Hanford experience: flow-sheet precedents, ion-exchange performance expectations, and glass-composition envelope questions can be reasoned from the specific Hanford analogs listed, with the analyte-level comparison as the bridge. The edge-of-cluster findings (salt batches 8 and 9 radiologically, all projected batches in salt chemistry) support targeted characterization: the model identifies which analytes drive the excess (the dashboard lists them per sample), which is precisely where additional SRS measurement effort buys the most cross-site confidence. The novel-stream finding (PRFT titanate sludges) sets an expectation: no Hanford process history will transfer cleanly, and treatability must be established from SRS data. The audit finding (all 51 closure residuals at the rim, salt projection impossible) disqualifies a class of comparisons: any claim of the form 'SRS residual X resembles Hanford tank Y' built on closure-residual data should be presumed to be a basis artifact until proven otherwise. And the missing-data finding (SB10's slurry report lacking all 25 element-panel analytes) is an action item with a name on it: the analog screen is only as complete as the reports it consumes, and it prices each gap immediately."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "4.4 Running the screen for a new batch"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The pipeline is deterministic and re-runnable end to end. To screen a future SRNL qualification sample: add the report to the measured-sample loader, rebuild the feature tables (scripts/build_unsupervised_features.py), re-run the clustering script (scripts/run_unsupervised_clustering.py; the taxonomy refits deterministically on the same Hanford inputs, and results land in data_processed/unsupervised/), and open the dashboard's SRS-projections tab. The whole loop is minutes, and because Hanford's taxonomy changes only when the BBI changes, results across batches are comparable over time. Two disciplines keep the comparisons honest: never mix bases within a panel comparison (current with current, closure with closure), and always report k with every distance."},
]

CAVEATS = [
    {"t": "h1", "text": "5. Caveats and limitations"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The method measures compositional similarity, nothing else. Processability, rheology, organic content, hydrogen generation, criticality margins, and heat load per unit volume depend on properties absent from public tank data, and no conclusion in this paper speaks to them. The specific limitations, in descending order of practical weight: (1) Basis asymmetry: Hanford BBI values are model-and-sample estimates with their own uncertainty and effective-date spread (2015 to 2026), while SRS current-waste values are direct measurements of qualification batches; part of every cross-site distance is this estimation-versus-measurement difference, unquantifiable from public data. (2) Non-detect sensitivity: the neutral-point rule is principled but not unique; a different imputation shifts distances by amounts that grow as support k shrinks, which is a second reason k travels with every number. (3) Feature selection: the presence floors and the interpretability window K = 4-9 are documented choices, not discoveries; the flat silhouette landscape means a different defensible K changes cluster membership at the margins (the Ward-KMeans ARI of 0.71 to 0.79 bounds how much). (4) Small supports: element-panel analogs on k=3 or 4 (SB11) carry little evidential weight individually. (5) Single-report samples: an SRS stream is characterized by one report on one basis; Hanford points aggregate decades of estimates. (6) GLOSH asymmetry: element-panel anomaly flags are unreliable as evidence (Section 3.6). (7) UMAP maps: useful for orientation, never for numbers. (8) Panel non-independence: the three panels describe the same tanks, so 'analog in two panels' is stronger than 'analog in one' but is not two independent confirmations."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "One meta-caveat belongs in its own sentence: the taxonomy is fitted to Hanford, so any family structure it finds is conditional on Hanford's fleet being the reference library. A taxonomy fitted jointly on both sites would produce different families, and a third site added later would change them again. The audit orientation (Hanford as library, SRS as query) is a choice that matches the question, not a claim that Hanford defines what tank waste is."},
]

CONCLUSIONS = [
    {"t": "h1", "text": "6. Conclusions"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "An unsupervised taxonomy of 433 Hanford tank-phase compositions, built in three chemistry panels with documented model selection, places every measured SRS processing stream somewhere specific: inside Hanford's own compositional spread in radionuclide space for the SWPF-AL-era salt batches (analog distances 2.6 to 3.4 on 7 to 9 shared nuclides), at the edge of the spread for the older filtered batches (5.4 to 6.7 on 19 shared nuclides) and for salt-chemistry comparisons generally (3.8 to 5.4 against a 1.45 median), and outside the Hanford library entirely for the titanate-bearing filter-follow sludges (11.8 to 12.2, driven by monosodium titanate at 12,200 to 16,225 micrograms per gram). The calibration the method supplies for free (each Hanford point's nearest same-cluster neighbor distance) turns these placements into calibrated statements: strong, moderate, directional, or novel."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The same machinery audits the data bases it consumes. It flags one genuinely peculiar Hanford point in radionuclide space (AW-104), exposes two SRS reporting gaps by name (Sludge Batch 10's slurry basis in element space; the closure basis in salt space), and places all 51 SRS closure residuals at or beyond the 95th percentile of their assigned clusters, a quantitative, label-free confirmation that closure residuals are not current waste and must not be compared against current inventories."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "For practitioners, the operating rules are short. Read k, then percentile, then analog identity, then distance. Trust radionuclide-panel anomaly flags; treat element-panel flags as prompts. Treat a below-median, k-7-plus analog as actionable; treat an above-p90, sub-5-k analog as indicative; treat a flagged-novel stream as a no-referent stream. Re-run the screen on every new qualification report; it costs minutes and prices every new gap immediately. The dashboard ships with the repository so that every number in this paper is two clicks from its underlying table."},
]

